{
  "requirement": "An execution error returns `Err` rather than a `Fingerprinted` containing a\ncompleted answer.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The text of `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.validate_node.errors` is: \"An execution error returns `Err` rather than a `Fingerprinted` containing a completed answer.\" It states only what a caller observes when execution fails. It names no library, algorithm, type, field or delegation interface. It also doesn't prescribe a signature.\n\nI considered one candidate structural obligation: a `Result`-returning signature. An implementation could use a different error-propagation mechanism, such as `?`, explicit matching, or a differently factored helper. All of these give the same observable outcome: the caller gets `Err` and no `Fingerprinted` answer. An implementation that returned `Ok(Fingerprinted(..))` carrying an error as a completed answer would violate the clause. That is a behavioral difference, and tests cover it.\n\nThe parent `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.validate_node` carries the structural obligations: the fresh `ValidationContext`, the call to `Criterion::execute`, and the returned `Fingerprinted`. This child adds no structural clause of its own.\n\nThere are no implementation roots, and none are needed. No structural obligation needs implementation evidence here.\n\nbehavioral: The obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.validate_node.errors` is that an execution error returns `Err`, not a `Fingerprinted` that holds a completed answer. Each error class is exercised by a test that asserts `Err` and, in most cases, that nothing was stored.\n\n- **Missing agent:** `agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors]` asserts `Err(ValidationError::Provider(_))` for a missing credential. `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls]` asserts `Err(NoAgentConfigured)` with no records stored.\n- **Agent failure:** `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[invalid_typed_submission_is_an_agent_error]` asserts `Err(Agent)` from both `ctx.ask` and `verify_with_agent`, and that no records were stored.\n- **Graph-side errors during execution:** `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[resolution_storage_and_callback_errors_are_not_judgments]` asserts `Err(Graph(\"missing\"))` for a read inside the callback. `tests/contracts.rs#fn[loading_execution_resolution_and_saving_failures_do_not_publish_answers]` asserts `Err(Graph(\"execute\"))`, `Err(Graph(\"missing\"))` and `Err(Graph(\"edges\"))`, with no records stored and no writes.\n- **Output decoding or encoding errors:** `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[criterion_decode_errors_are_execution_errors_and_false_is_a_completed_answer]` asserts that non-JSON input gives `Err(Output(syntax))` and stores nothing. It also contrasts this with a completed `false` answer, which is `Ok`. `tests/contracts.rs#fn[serialization_failure_leaves_the_existing_record_unchanged]` asserts `Err(Output)` on a failed encode and that the existing record is unchanged.\n- **Error variants on load:** `tests/contracts.rs#fn[typed_loading_reports_decode_errors_even_for_stale_evidence]` and `tests/storage.rs#fn[malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors]` assert `Err` for decode and storage errors. They also assert that a fresh execution afterwards still returns `Ok`.\n\nTogether these cover the error and success contrast, and the assertions enforce the `Err` outcome. I found no unmarked relevant tests and no unaddressed obligation.",
